{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 55, :comments ["<b>And all the land of Egypt was famished.</b> In the second year. This teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, called to the famine, in the manner of what is written (II Kings 8:1): ״כי קרא ה׳ לרעב״ — for HaShem had called to the famine. And it is written (Psalms 105:16–17): ״ויקרא רעב על הארץ כל מטה לחם שבר״ — and He called famine upon the land; every staff of bread He broke. And because of the famine, ״ויצעק העם אל פרעה ללחם״ — the people cried out to Pharaoh for bread; so is the way of the starving, to call out and cry to whoever has it in his hand to satisfy them, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 107:5–6): ״רעבים גם צמאים נפשם בהם תתעטף״ — hungry and thirsty, their soul faints in them; and it is written: ״ויצעקו אל ה׳ בצר להם״ — and they cried to HaShem in their distress." "<b>Go to Joseph.</b> For he is in my place for every matter, and I have nothing left but the throne; therefore you must do whatever he tells you." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'Go to Joseph' — the Egyptians went to Joseph and said to him, 'Give us bread.' He said to them, 'Go and circumcise yourselves and I will give you bread.' They went to Pharaoh and wept before him; they said to him, 'We went to Joseph and he speaks to us empty words and tells us to circumcise ourselves.' Pharaoh said to them, 'Fools, why do you cry to me? Why did you not set aside grain from the third year and the fourth year?' They said to him, 'All the grain in our houses rotted.' He said to them, 'Not even a loaf of bread remained from yesterday.' He said to them, 'Even the bread that was set in the basket rotted.' He said to them, 'Go to Joseph; whatever he tells you, do.' This is what the verse says (Proverbs 11:26): ״מונע בר יקבוהו לאום וברכה לראש משביר״ — he who withholds grain, the people will curse him, but blessing is on the head of the provider. 'He who withholds grain' — that is Pharaoh. 'Blessing is on the head of the provider' — that is Joseph, as it is said (Genesis 49:26): ״ברכות אביך גברו על ברכות הורי״ — the blessings of your father prevailed over the blessings of my progenitors, etc.; ״תבואתה לראש יוסף״ — let it come on the head of Joseph."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:48.895487823Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}